  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nAbove-normal temperatures will persist across eastern Idaho for\nthe next few days as the region remains positioned beneath a \nbroad southwesterly upper-level flow regime. High-resolution \nconvective-allowing models (CAMs) continue to show low-end \nprobabilities for isolated showers and embedded thunderstorms \ndeveloping during peak heating, though most areas will remain \ndry. Afternoon highs in lower-elevation valleys will hold in the\nupper 60s to lower 70s through Thursday.\n\nThe driver for our next major pattern shift will move into the \ncentral California coast on Wednesday before shifting \nnortheastward toward the northern Great Basin, eventually \ntracking directly overhead by Friday. Significant 500 mb height \nfalls and strong dynamic lift associated with this incoming low \nwill bring widespread precipitation and a sharp airmass \ntransition to eastern Idaho.\n\nThe most favorable window for widespread precipitation runs \nfrom late Thursday evening into the early morning hours on \nSaturday. QPF guidance has remained consistent with recent runs:\nexpect around 1.0 inch across much of the Snake Plain, 0.50 inch\nin the Magic Valley, 0.50 to 1.0 inch around Sun Valley due to\nfavorable upslope enhancement, and 1.0 to 1.5 inches across the\nEastern Highlands although some locally higher rainfall totals\nare certainly possible and these forecast amounts could still\nchange over the next couple of forecasts. This setup will \nproduce another widespread soaking rainfall event across the \nregion. In addition to the precipitation, strong cold advection \nand persistent cloud cover will limit Friday daytime highs \nstrictly to the 40s across the entire forecast area.\n\nPrecipitation will taper off from west to east during the early\nhours of Saturday morning. Temperatures will remain well below \nseasonal norms through the weekend, holding daytime highs in the\n50s. A slow moderating trend will take place heading into early\nnext week, though afternoon maximums will likely stay at or \njust below seasonal averages, keeping conditions noticeably \ncooler than the unseasonable warmth observed early this week.",
